    "lawCode": "Amd §208, Tax L; amd §11-652, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8991\n\nSPONSOR: FLANAGAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the tax law and the administrative\ncode of the city of New York, in relation to the definitions of the\nterms \"CFC income\" and \"entire net income\"\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would prevent an unintended corporate tax increase on\nglobal intangible low-taxed income (GILTI) resulting from the passage of\nthe Federal Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 6-a of section 208 of the tax law to amend\nthe definition of \"exempt CFC income\" to include income required to be\nincluded in a taxpayer's Federal gross income pursuant to subsection (a)\nof section 951A of the internal revenue code, without regard to the\ndeduction under section 250 of the internal revenue code.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 9 of section 208 of the tax law by adding a\nnew subparagraph to include the amount of any Federal deduction allowed\npursuant to section 250 (a) (1) (B) (i) of the internal revenue code.\n\nSection 3 amends subdivision 5-a of section 11-652 of the administrative\ncode of the city of New York to amend the definition of \"exempt CFC\nincome\" to include income required to be included in a taxpayer's Feder-\nal gross income pursuant to subsection (a) of section 951A of the inter-\nnal revenue code, without regard to the deduction under section 250 of\nthe internal revenue code.\n\nSection 4 amends subdivision 8 of section 11-652 of the administrative\ncode of the city of New York by adding a new subparagraph to include the\namount of any Federal deduction allowed pursuant to section 250 (a) (1)\n(B) (i) of the internal revenue code.\n\nSection 5 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs a result of the passage of the Federal Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017,\nchanges were made to tax treatment of income producing foreign held\nintangible assets. These changes were intended to address corporations\nholding intangible assets, such as patents, copyrights, trademarks, etc.\nin low tax countries as a means of reducing their tax burden, and to\nencourage the repatriation of those assets.  However, a technical change\n(the creation of a new section of the internal revenue code- 951A\nreplaced section 951(a)) resulted in other types of intangible income,\nsuch as financial transactions, being included in this section, which\nresults in the New York State exemption for foreign financial trans-\nactions being eliminated, as the existing exemption still refers to\nsection 951(a) of the internal revenue code.\n\nIf the Federal government had not passed the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of\n2017, the income that is now treated under 951A of the internal revenue\ncode and thus subject to New York State and City taxation would have\nbeen tax exempt. This bill is merely seeking to return to the same\ntreatment of CFC income that had been in place in New York for decades.\nThe financial services industry is one of the largest industries in New\nYork, and the passage of this bill would prevent an unintended tax\nincrease on the industry, and would prevent New York State from receiv-\ning a windfall as a result of Federal tax changes, which is consistent\nwith actions approved in a bipartisan fashion as part of the passage of\nthe FY 2019 Enacted Budget.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill would have no fiscal impact, as it would preserve the New York\ntax treatment of CFC income that was in effect prior to the enactment of\nthe Federal Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately, and apply to tax years begin-\nning on or after January 1, 2018.\n",
